#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069899} :activity/id #uuid "afe6da6e-0855-538d-ac54-1e34f990246c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489 :topic/id #uuid "4d6f30e9-3dde-50ef-bfb2-830878d94eaf" :topic/math-academy-id 478} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070016 :tutorial/content "We know how to integrate $\\cos{x}.$ But how do we integrate $\\cos{3x}?$ \n\nTo compute\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos{3x} \\, \\textrm dx,\n$$\n\nwe can use substitution. If we let $u=3x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** After solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+C) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "075eaa34-761d-56ce-92f5-b498b52b92ac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 854 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9a303692-65c8-5fbf-8f20-8a7e6213e724" :step/math-academy-id 617129 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069900}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068915 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068567 :question/id #uuid "0021ae6a-8ef8-564e-9c2e-fb5f9e8f7313" :question/math-academy-id "e-464"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "746d1bc6-98ec-59a1-bec6-9dcfb7081a5d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048777}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429932}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"} :step/id #uuid "2fa264e5-2795-5ecb-81bd-f24706045bc7" :step/math-academy-id 617130 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069901}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068916 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068568 :question/id #uuid "daf5cc74-4c7d-500a-9345-03bd6b73a333" :question/math-academy-id "e-465"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084eb4cc-2f84-5288-90a6-be84066922b6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429933}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions With Linear Arguments"} :step/id #uuid "06591b79-5220-5187-8baa-3fb3da419824" :step/math-academy-id 617133 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070017 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 2x \\cos{(x^2)}\\,\\textrm d x.$\n\nNotice that if we let $u = x^2,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\underbrace{2x}_{u'} \\cos({\\overbrace{x^2}^u})\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the factor multiplying the trigonometric function is the derivative of the argument! Integrals like this can always be solved using substitution.\n\nSo, let $u=x^2.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d u = 2x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f981f86c-ae3e-5137-b347-764c79c6eb45" :tutorial/math-academy-id 857 :tutorial/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "ac0f9fbe-d73f-5211-9b09-02cf5b0b4700" :step/math-academy-id 617136 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068917 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068569 :question/id #uuid "234c7a86-037c-5316-9419-14db90d1da60" :question/math-academy-id "e-466"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d359337c-4ec2-5100-bdac-d0858619db3b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319367}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Products Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "49d49ff9-a1d0-509a-8981-81289b3ebc93" :step/math-academy-id 617137 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068918 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068570 :question/id #uuid "03e461a3-0fe0-5fb5-813e-ae23a5dc606a" :question/math-academy-id "e-467"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc24eade-1b3d-5672-88fd-0bb48b371509" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429930}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Quotients Containing Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "590cad06-7d45-5569-8593-720ef7930553" :step/math-academy-id 617140}] :activity/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186069001}]}
